{"name":"Hell Rider","hex":"#c40700","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hell","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A place of torment where some or all sinners are believed to go after death and evil spirits are believed to be.","Synonym of underworld, an underground land of the dead without eternal punishment or with only some regions of punishment."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place or situation of great suffering in life.","A place for gambling."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hell"},{"term":"rider","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mounted person.\n A knight, or other mounted warrior. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\nAn old Dutch gold coin with the figure of a man on horseback stamped upon it. \n\n Someone who rides a horse or (later) a bicycle, motorcycle etc. \n\n A motorcycle enthusiast; a motorcycle club member.\n A mounted robber; a bandit, especially in the Scottish borders. \n\n Someone who breaks in or manages a horse; a riding master. \n\n An agent who goes out with samples of goods to obtain orders; a commercial traveller or travelling salesman. \n\n  Someone riding in a vehicle; a passenger on public transport. \n\n A boarder, a person who rides a board in boardsports.\n A skateboarder\n\n A snowboarder","A knight, or other mounted warrior. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rider"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hell-Rider","blocks":[{"text":"Hell-Rider is a short-lived, black-and-white comics magazine published by Skywald Publications, a 1970s company best known for its horror comics magazines Nightmare, Psycho, and Scream. Like them and the similar publications of Warren Publishing, these were mature-audience magazines not covered by comic books' Comics Code Authority. Its cover price was 60¢, typical for that format and time, during which standard comic books sold for 15¢."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Hell-Rider_1.pdf/page1-330px-Hell-Rider_1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hell-Rider"},{"title":"Hell","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and folklore, Hell is a location or state in the afterlife in which souls are subjected to punishment after death. Religions with a linear divine history sometimes depict hells as eternal, such as in some versions of Christianity and Islam, whereas religions with reincarnation usually depict a hell as an intermediary period between incarnations, as is the case in the Indian religions. Religions typically locate Hell in another dimension or under Earth's surface. Other afterlife destinations include heaven, paradise, purgatory, limbo, and the underworld."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Fra_Angelico_010.jpg/330px-Fra_Angelico_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hell"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Hell-Rider_1.pdf/page1-500px-Hell-Rider_1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Hell-Rider_1.pdf/page1-500px-Hell-Rider_1.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHell-Rider_1.pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/HellRider_panel.jpg/500px-HellRider_panel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/HellRider_panel.jpg/500px-HellRider_panel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Panel from Hell-Rider #1, art by Ross Andru and Mike Esposito. The blood-spewing violence was graphic for the time.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHellRider_panel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Hel_%281889%29_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg/500px-Hel_%281889%29_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Hel_%281889%29_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg/500px-Hel_%281889%29_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hel (1889) by Johannes Gehrts, depicts the Old Norse Hel, a goddess-like figure, in the location of the same name, which she oversees","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHel_(1889)_by_Johannes_Gehrts.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG/500px-Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG/1280px-Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG/500px-Fresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Preserved colonial wall paintings of 1802 depicting Hell,[14][15][16] by Tadeo Escalante, inside the Church of San Juan Bautista in Huaro, Peru","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFresque_%C3%A9glise_huaro.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg/500px-Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg/1280px-Dumuzi_aux_enfers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f